# Arch Linux build artifacts
*.pkg.tar.*
*.src.tar.*

# Extracted sources and build directories
src/
pkg/

# Downloaded source archives
*.tar.gz
*.tar.xz
*.tar.bz2
*.zip
*.tar.zst

# Logs
*.log

# Other artifacts
logpipe.*

# Handoff directory
handoffs/

# nvchecker local state
keyfile.toml

# Python bytecode cache
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# Background process output
nohup.out

# Tool config
.opencode/

# Generated wrapper artifacts
packages/*/package.pkl
packages/*/package.json
manifest.json

# SRCINFO as build artifact (per SRCINFO-VERSION-CONTROL-POLICY.md)
packages/*/.SRCINFO

# Ignore AUR .git metadata 
packages/*/.git

# makepkg bare clones for git+ sources. makepkg checks out <name>/ in the
# PKGBUILD's directory (and src/<name>/ for the worktree), where <name> is the
# upstream repo name, not the package name. amass-git gets packages/amass-git/
# amass/ and opendoas gets packages/opendoas/opendoas/. Without this, a
# discovery run stages a multi-hundred-MB clone and the push is rejected.
#
# The inner `*` is required. `packages/*/*/.git/` cannot do it: the pattern
# matches the directory named .git, not the clone's contents, and listing only
# the clone ROOT in the catch-all's run-off cannot help either -- git does not
# re-descend into an excluded directory, so a deeper negation never takes
# effect. Excluding the contents here is what makes the root stay excluded.
packages/*/*/*
packages/*/*/.git/
packages/amass-git/amass/
packages/opendoas/opendoas/

# sync-package.sh's merge baseline. It downloads the upstream PKGBUILD to
# PKGBUILD.new and keeps a copy as .PKGBUILD.upstream to detect drift on the
# next run; it is fetched afresh each time, never needed from history, and no
# commit in this repository has ever tracked one.
packages/*/.PKGBUILD.upstream

# Downloaded source= payloads. sync-package.sh's fetch_upstream_assets()
# pulls any source entry named with the local-name form (foo::https://...) into
# the package directory. These are build inputs, reproducible from the URL in
# source=, so they do not belong in history.
#
# Enumerating exact names here does not scale: a new package's payloads are
# unknown until its first sync, and the enumeration has already missed files
# twice (the per-arch regal_Linux_* pair, and fantomas-bin's four). The rule
# below is derived from the one invariant that does hold -- a repo-local source
# is always declared as a bare filename with no URL, so every other source file
# in a package directory arrived over the network and is reproducible.
#
# packages/*/ holds the PKGBUILD (a tracked repo file) and the generated
# package.pkl/package.json (ignored above). Anything else in a package
# directory is downloaded. Keep a new repo-local file by adding a negation in
# the correct position -- see the ordering note below.
#
# The two documented exceptions are makepkg's bare clones for git+ sources
# (listed above so a rebuild does not re-download them) and .PKGBUILD.upstream
# (sync-package.sh reads it as a merge baseline). Both are re-included here.
#
# ONLY the declared source files come back. Markdown and data files are
# deliberately excluded: every one such file in this repository today is a
# downloaded upstream document (README.md, changelog.md, ...-LICENSE.md,
# SECURITY.md from a source entry), never a repo-local file. The repo-local
# sources are scripts (.sh, .py) and patches, which the negations below restore.
#
# .PKGBUILD.upstream is likewise NOT restored: it is a download, not a declared
# source, so it stays excluded by the catch-all above.
packages/*/*
!packages/*/*/
!packages/*/PKGBUILD
!packages/*/PKGBUILD.common
!packages/*/PKGBUILD.new
!packages/*/.SRCINFO
!packages/*/.git
!packages/*/.nvchecker.toml
!packages/*/.nvchecker.toml.disabled
!packages/*/*.sh
!packages/*/*.py
!packages/*/*.patch
!packages/*/*.install
!packages/*/*.desktop
!packages/*/*.service
!packages/*/*.conf

# A package directory holding a nested tree of its own tracked files opts out of
# the catch-all above, so it keeps normal, enumerated .gitignore semantics. The
# rules below are the ones that would otherwise be lost; a package that takes
# this route keeps its payloads covered here instead.
#
# ranger-doas is the first such package (it carries policy_exceptions.yaml plus
# several .patch files). Its upstream helper files are enumerated because the
# generated source names do not all end in a recognizable extension.
packages/ranger-doas/*
!packages/ranger-doas/*/
!packages/ranger-doas/PKGBUILD
!packages/ranger-doas/*.patch
!packages/ranger-doas/*.install
!packages/ranger-doas/*.sh
!packages/ranger-doas/*.py
!packages/ranger-doas/*.yaml
!packages/ranger-doas/*.md
!packages/ranger-doas/.nvchecker.toml
!packages/ranger-doas/.nvchecker.toml.disabled
!packages/ranger-doas/.SRCINFO
packages/ranger-doas/doas-substitution.patch.orig
packages/ranger-doas/ranger_refs.bin
packages/ranger-doas/*.rej
packages/ranger-doas/*.orig

# Global artifact patterns, last so that no negation above can re-include one.
# Git applies the last matching pattern, and a negation always wins over an
# earlier exclusion, so artifact patterns must come AFTER the negations:
# placed before them, `*.log` would be re-included by `!packages/*/*.py`... and
# by any other negation matching the same path.
*.tgz
*.mmdb
*.log
*.pkg.tar.*
*.src.tar.*
*.tar.gz
*.tar.xz
*.tar.bz2
*.zip
*.tar.zst
